1
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:46,371
A third of the
land on our planet is desert

2
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:58,818
These great scars
on the face of the earth

3
00:00:58,858 --> 00:01:00,617
appear to
be lifeless

4
00:01:00,657 --> 00:01:02,096
But surprisingly

5
00:01:02,136 --> 00:01:03,576
none are

6
00:01:10,526 --> 00:01:11,648
In all of them

7
00:01:11,688 --> 00:01:13,731
life manages somehow

8
00:01:13,771 --> 00:01:16,294
to keep a
precarious hold

9
00:01:37,519 --> 00:01:40,080
Not all
deserts are hot

10
00:01:40,441 --> 00:01:44,042
50 mile an hour
winds blowing in from Siberia

11
00:01:44,082 --> 00:01:47,843
bring snow to the
Gobi Desert in Mongolia

12
00:02:14,110 --> 00:02:17,671
From a summer
high of 50 degrees centigrade

13
00:02:17,711 --> 00:02:21,512
the temperature
in midwinter can drop to minus 40

14
00:02:22,032 --> 00:02:25,873
making this one
of the harshest deserts of all

15
00:02:34,993 --> 00:02:39,995
Few animals can
survive these extreme changes

16
00:02:46,438 --> 00:02:48,959
Wild Bactrian camels

17
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,641
one of the rarest
mammals on the planet

18
00:02:51,801 --> 00:02:54,402
And perhaps
the hardiest

19
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:58,330
Their biggest
problem is the lack of water

20
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:00,130
particularly
now in winter

21
00:03:00,170 --> 00:03:03,370
when the little
there is is locked up as ice

22
00:03:10,649 --> 00:03:11,849
Surprisingly

23
00:03:11,889 --> 00:03:14,689
snow here
never melts

24
00:03:15,009 --> 00:03:19,609
The air is just
too cold and too dry for it to do so

25
00:03:25,019 --> 00:03:28,742
The sun's rays
turn it straight into vapour

26
00:03:28,863 --> 00:03:30,624
It evaporates

27
00:03:38,873 --> 00:03:42,196
But it is the
only source of water

28
00:03:42,277 --> 00:03:45,119
So Bactrian
camels eat it

29
00:03:46,102 --> 00:03:47,542
Elsewhere
in the world

30
00:03:47,582 --> 00:03:51,742
a camel at a waterhole can drink as much as 200 litres

31
00:03:51,782 --> 00:03:53,582
during a
single visit

32
00:03:56,142 --> 00:03:56,742
Here

33
00:03:56,782 --> 00:03:59,822
the strategy is
to take little and often

34
00:03:59,862 --> 00:04:01,302
and with
good reason

35
00:04:01,342 --> 00:04:03,582
For filling the
stomach with snow

36
00:04:03,622 --> 00:04:04,942
could be fatal

37
00:04:10,558 --> 00:04:12,798
The camels must
limit themselves

38
00:04:12,838 --> 00:04:16,559
to the equivalent
of just ten litres a day

39
00:04:33,362 --> 00:04:36,323
Winter is the
time for breeding

40
00:04:40,997 --> 00:04:43,117
This extraordinary performance

41
00:04:43,157 --> 00:04:45,437
is a male
Bactrian camel's way

42
00:04:45,477 --> 00:04:48,957
of attracting the
attention of a passing female

43
00:04:50,996 --> 00:04:51,756
In summer

44
00:04:51,796 --> 00:04:55,316
the camels can't
stray far from waterholes

45
00:04:55,956 --> 00:04:56,836
But now

46
00:04:56,876 --> 00:04:59,396
with mouthfuls of
snow lying everywhere

47
00:04:59,436 --> 00:05:02,515
They can travel
widely in search of mates

48
00:05:06,950 --> 00:05:07,711
Today

49
00:05:07,751 --> 00:05:11,195
less than a thousand of these desert specialists

50
00:05:11,235 --> 00:05:13,037
remain in the wild

51
00:05:13,398 --> 00:05:14,439
The Gobi

52
00:05:14,479 --> 00:05:16,201
hostile
though it is

53
00:05:16,241 --> 00:05:18,684
is their
last stronghold

54
00:05:25,172 --> 00:05:28,575
There is no other
desert quite like the Gobi

55
00:05:28,736 --> 00:05:31,539
But why is this
place a desert？

56
00:05:33,016 --> 00:05:36,139
There is one
simple and massive cause

57
00:05:36,260 --> 00:05:37,822
the Himalayas

58
00:05:42,469 --> 00:05:46,795
Clouds blowing
from the south hit this gigantic barrier

59
00:05:46,835 --> 00:05:48,317
As they're
forced upwards

60
00:05:48,357 --> 00:05:51,722
so they empty
their moisture on the mountain slopes

61
00:05:51,762 --> 00:05:55,246
Leaving little
for the land on the other side

62
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:01,450
From space

63
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:04,170
deserts are
very conspicuous

64
00:06:06,849 --> 00:06:11,569
Dunes of sand hundreds of miles long streak their surface

65
00:06:16,599 --> 00:06:19,883
With no cloak of
vegetation to conceal them

66
00:06:19,923 --> 00:06:24,127
Strange formations are exposed in the naked rock

67
00:06:30,414 --> 00:06:34,257
Africa's Sahara
is the largest desert of all

68
00:06:34,298 --> 00:06:36,701
It's the size of
the United States

69
00:06:36,741 --> 00:06:39,263
And the biggest
source of sand and dust

70
00:06:39,303 --> 00:06:41,265
In the
entire world

71
00:07:21,843 --> 00:07:25,243
Sandstorms like
these appear without warning

72
00:07:25,283 --> 00:07:29,962
And reduce visibility for days over areas the size of Britain

73
00:07:39,334 --> 00:07:40,335
Dromedaries

74
00:07:40,375 --> 00:07:42,015
single
humped camels

75
00:07:42,055 --> 00:07:44,576
take these
storms in their stride

76
00:07:48,178 --> 00:07:52,619
The heavy sand
rises only a few metres above the ground

77
00:07:52,659 --> 00:07:57,861
But the dust can
be blown 5000 metres up into the sky

78
00:08:03,316 --> 00:08:04,959
The ferocious wind

79
00:08:04,999 --> 00:08:07,201
armed with
grains of sand

80
00:08:07,241 --> 00:08:10,685
is the agent that
shapes all deserts

81
00:08:20,251 --> 00:08:23,493
Reptiles have
armoured， scaly skins

82
00:08:23,533 --> 00:08:26,535
that protect them
from the stinging grazes

83
00:08:29,417 --> 00:08:30,618
For insects

84
00:08:30,658 --> 00:08:34,100
the bombardment
can be very severe indeed

85
00:08:36,302 --> 00:08:39,544
The only escape
is below the surface

86
00:08:56,566 --> 00:08:59,288
As the winds
rise and fall

87
00:08:59,328 --> 00:09:00,889
swirl and eddy

88
00:09:00,929 --> 00:09:04,051
So they pile the
sand into dunes

89
00:09:08,094 --> 00:09:12,056
These sand seas
can be hundreds of miles across

90
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:17,300
In Namibia

91
00:09:17,340 --> 00:09:21,103
the winds have
built some of the biggest dunes in the world

92
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:33,606
Star dunes like
these can be 300 metres high

93
00:09:33,646 --> 00:09:36,009
Grains swept
up the flanks

94
00:09:36,049 --> 00:09:38,372
are blown off the
crests of the ridges

95
00:09:38,412 --> 00:09:41,135
So it's only the
tops that are moving

96
00:09:48,071 --> 00:09:52,360
The main body of
these dunes may not have shifted for 5000 years

97
00:10:05,796 --> 00:10:09,480
Few rocks can
resist the continuous blast

98
00:10:09,520 --> 00:10:11,762
of the sand
carrying wind

99
00:10:12,324 --> 00:10:16,568
These outcrops
are standing in Egypt's white desert

100
00:10:17,610 --> 00:10:20,613
But they will not
do so for much longer

101
00:10:22,176 --> 00:10:24,699
They're being
inexorably chiselled away

102
00:10:24,739 --> 00:10:27,541
and turned
into more sand

103
00:11:07,089 --> 00:11:07,810
Now

104
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:11,735
lumps of heavily
eroded rocks have been marooned

105
00:11:11,775 --> 00:11:13,937
in a sea of sand

106
00:11:31,378 --> 00:11:33,538
These
jagged pyramids

107
00:11:33,578 --> 00:11:35,258
100 metres tall

108
00:11:35,298 --> 00:11:38,858
were once part of
a continuous rocky plateau

109
00:11:38,978 --> 00:11:43,698
The blasting sand
will eventually eliminate them altogether

110
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:54,240
The relentless
power of the wind

111
00:11:54,280 --> 00:11:58,399
ensures that the
face of a desert is continually changing

112
00:11:58,479 --> 00:12:01,278
But there is one
constant presence

113
00:12:09,636 --> 00:12:11,276
The desert sun

114
00:12:26,346 --> 00:12:29,991
The sun's heat
and power to evaporate water

115
00:12:30,031 --> 00:12:33,236
has had a profound effect on the bodies and habits

116
00:12:33,276 --> 00:12:35,519
of everything
that lives here

117
00:12:40,046 --> 00:12:43,410
This sun
potentially is a killer

118
00:12:44,854 --> 00:12:48,498
And the red
kangaroos must acknowledge that

119
00:12:50,028 --> 00:12:50,789
Right now

120
00:12:50,829 --> 00:12:52,312
while the
sun is low

121
00:12:52,352 --> 00:12:55,276
there's no
immediate cause for concern

122
00:12:56,238 --> 00:12:59,363
But this
situation won't last long

123
00:13:00,366 --> 00:13:03,571
Australia is the
world's most arid continent

124
00:13:03,611 --> 00:13:06,575
With blistering
daytime temperatures

125
00:13:13,787 --> 00:13:14,668
Every hour

126
00:13:14,708 --> 00:13:19,233
the temperature
rises by five degrees centigrade

127
00:13:26,443 --> 00:13:27,004
Soon

128
00:13:27,044 --> 00:13:30,207
the heat will
reach a critical point

129
00:13:40,338 --> 00:13:42,498
Any kangaroo
out in the open

130
00:13:42,538 --> 00:13:45,939
is in serious
danger of overheating

131
00:13:51,939 --> 00:13:53,220
In the full sun

132
00:13:53,260 --> 00:13:57,860
the temperature
on the ground soars to 70 degrees

133
00:14:02,755 --> 00:14:03,757
By midday

134
00:14:03,797 --> 00:14:06,043
the radiation
is so intense

135
00:14:06,083 --> 00:14:08,406
they must
take shelter

136
00:14:28,820 --> 00:14:29,859
In the shade

137
00:14:29,899 --> 00:14:32,459
they're shielded
from much of the sun's energy

138
00:14:32,499 --> 00:14:35,339
But their body
temperature can still rise

139
00:14:38,218 --> 00:14:41,817
So they lick
saliva onto their forearms

140
00:14:41,857 --> 00:14:46,096
Where there is a network of blood vessels close to the surface of the skin

141
00:14:46,136 --> 00:14:48,656
And as the
saliva evaporates

142
00:14:48,696 --> 00:14:50,695
their
blood is cooled

143
00:14:53,548 --> 00:14:58,274
This thermal image shows just how effective the process is

144
00:14:58,314 --> 00:15:01,918
The blue areas on
the body are the cooler parts

145
00:15:11,250 --> 00:15:13,093
As the
saliva dries

146
00:15:13,133 --> 00:15:15,095
it has to
be replaced

147
00:15:15,135 --> 00:15:19,260
And this is a
real drain on the kangaroo's body fluids

148
00:15:29,867 --> 00:15:31,148
Even in the shade

149
00:15:31,188 --> 00:15:33,470
the earth
is baking hot

150
00:15:34,392 --> 00:15:37,916
So the kangaroos
dig away the warmed topsoil

151
00:15:37,956 --> 00:15:40,478
To get at the
cooler ground beneath

152
00:16:03,843 --> 00:16:05,645
By staying
in the shade

153
00:16:05,685 --> 00:16:08,529
and licking to
control their body temperature

154
00:16:08,569 --> 00:16:11,852
kangaroos manage
to get through the hottest part of the day

155
00:16:11,892 --> 00:16:13,654
without
heat stroke

156
00:16:14,695 --> 00:16:17,058
But for the
majority of desert animals

157
00:16:17,098 --> 00:16:20,501
this strategy
would not be enough for survival

158
00:16:43,779 --> 00:16:48,864
The extraordinary
ears of the panic foxes of Africa radiate heat

159
00:16:48,904 --> 00:16:52,106
But the animals
have another way of keeping cool

160
00:17:02,005 --> 00:17:04,485
They spend their
days underground

161
00:17:04,605 --> 00:17:07,365
and only
emerge at sunset

162
00:17:19,622 --> 00:17:22,945
Darkness
brings huge changes

163
00:17:23,186 --> 00:17:24,186
In the Sahara

164
00:17:24,226 --> 00:17:28,150
the temperature
can drop as much as 30 degrees during the night

165
00:17:28,190 --> 00:17:32,593
So it's cool enough to allow these desert fox cubs to play

166
00:17:40,561 --> 00:17:43,283
All sorts of
creatures now appear

167
00:17:43,323 --> 00:17:46,405
including some
really unexpected ones

168
00:17:49,508 --> 00:17:51,870
Toads have
permeable skins

169
00:17:51,910 --> 00:17:55,792
and would quickly
die from desiccation out in the daytime heat

170
00:17:55,912 --> 00:17:58,634
It's only now
that they can leave shelter

171
00:18:01,516 --> 00:18:03,718
The same is
true for scorpions

172
00:18:03,758 --> 00:18:06,919
even though their
shells are actually watertight

173
00:18:19,518 --> 00:18:20,239
In fact

174
00:18:20,279 --> 00:18:23,401
most small
desert creatures are nocturnal

175
00:18:23,441 --> 00:18:25,723
So it's only now
that you can judge

176
00:18:25,763 --> 00:18:29,365
just how much
life there can be in a desert

177
00:18:29,966 --> 00:18:32,689
But moisture
lost even at night

178
00:18:32,729 --> 00:18:34,770
has to be
replaced sometime

179
00:18:34,810 --> 00:18:35,851
somehow

180
00:18:35,891 --> 00:18:38,293
And that problem
dominates the lives

181
00:18:38,333 --> 00:18:40,454
of all
desert dwellers

182
00:18:48,824 --> 00:18:51,145
The Atacama in Chile

183
00:18:51,185 --> 00:18:54,466
This is the
driest desert in the world

184
00:18:56,388 --> 00:18:59,470
Some parts may
not see rain for 50 years

185
00:18:59,510 --> 00:19:00,950
And with
such a record

186
00:19:00,990 --> 00:19:04,312
you would expect
the place to be completely barren

187
00:19:09,836 --> 00:19:12,597
These are South
America's camels

188
00:19:12,637 --> 00:19:13,998
Huanacos

189
00:19:15,638 --> 00:19:18,121
They're very good
at conserving moisture

190
00:19:18,161 --> 00:19:21,444
but they nonetheless need a regular supply of water

191
00:19:34,539 --> 00:19:37,822
They get it
partly from cactus flowers

192
00:19:37,862 --> 00:19:41,586
But that explanation raises another question

193
00:19:43,855 --> 00:19:47,257
How do the cacti
survive without rain？

194
00:19:54,265 --> 00:19:58,989
Hot winds suck all the moisture from the surface of the land

195
00:20:00,951 --> 00:20:01,592
Clearly

196
00:20:01,632 --> 00:20:05,795
there must be something else that takes the place of rain

197
00:20:07,292 --> 00:20:12,936
The secret is a
cold sea current that runs parallel to the land

198
00:20:30,383 --> 00:20:33,864
The cold water
cools the moist warm air above it

199
00:20:33,904 --> 00:20:36,824
and that
produces banks of fog

200
00:21:00,269 --> 00:21:01,710
At the same time

201
00:21:01,750 --> 00:21:06,512
wind blowing
onto the shore sweeps the fog inland

202
00:21:22,202 --> 00:21:23,242
Before long

203
00:21:23,282 --> 00:21:26,444
the cacti are
dripping with dew

204
00:21:35,624 --> 00:21:37,583
The fog is
so regular

205
00:21:37,623 --> 00:21:41,263
that moistureloving lichens are able to grow on the cacti

206
00:21:41,303 --> 00:21:44,423
and they absorb
liquid like a sponge

207
00:21:47,182 --> 00:21:49,942
In a land of
almost no rain

208
00:21:49,982 --> 00:21:52,902
these precious
drops are lifesavers

209
00:21:52,942 --> 00:21:55,222
for many
different creatures

210
00:22:04,485 --> 00:22:05,847
Further inland

211
00:22:05,887 --> 00:22:07,969
the air
remains so warm

212
00:22:08,009 --> 00:22:10,412
that its moisture
does not condense

213
00:22:10,452 --> 00:22:12,654
So this slender
strip of desert

214
00:22:12,694 --> 00:22:15,337
is virtually the
only part of the Atacama

215
00:22:15,377 --> 00:22:17,219
where life
can exist

216
00:22:21,504 --> 00:22:23,426
And without the fog

217
00:22:23,707 --> 00:22:26,189
this land too
would be empty

218
00:22:27,982 --> 00:22:43,422
The huarnacos
make the most of the dew

219
00:22:43,742 --> 00:22:46,062
But it will not
remain for long

220
00:22:47,004 --> 00:22:48,165
In an hour or two

221
00:22:48,205 --> 00:22:49,966
the sun will
have burnt it off

222
00:22:50,006 --> 00:22:52,568
and dried the
surface of the cacti

223
00:23:01,536 --> 00:23:04,179
The Sonoran
desert in Arizona

224
00:23:04,219 --> 00:23:07,061
is not quite so
dry as the Atacama

225
00:23:07,101 --> 00:23:09,142
Some rain
does fall

226
00:23:10,287 --> 00:23:11,930
But it is
infrequent

227
00:23:11,970 --> 00:23:13,533
And when it
does arrive

228
00:23:13,573 --> 00:23:17,218
Animals and plants have to be ready to make the most of it

229
00:23:18,942 --> 00:23:20,424
And it's coming

230
00:23:29,236 --> 00:23:31,521
When the summer
monsoon blows in

231
00:23:31,561 --> 00:23:33,044
The giant saguaro

232
00:23:33,084 --> 00:23:35,168
one of the
biggest of all cacti

233
00:23:35,208 --> 00:23:37,852
Are ready to take
full advantage of it

234
00:24:12,235 --> 00:24:13,957
After a rainstorm

235
00:24:13,997 --> 00:24:18,681
the sawaow's long
shallow root system sucks up the water

236
00:24:19,123 --> 00:24:23,847
And the pleats on
its trunk enable it to expand rapidly

237
00:24:31,921 --> 00:24:33,082
When full

238
00:24:33,122 --> 00:24:37,445
a sawara stem
can store up to five tons of water

239
00:24:37,485 --> 00:24:41,328
And that's enough
to see it through many months of drought

240
00:24:46,212 --> 00:24:48,494
The trunks of
these huge plants

241
00:24:48,534 --> 00:24:51,696
provide homes for
the keela woodpecker

242
00:24:54,042 --> 00:24:57,690
But birds are not
the only animals to benefit

243
00:24:57,730 --> 00:25:00,014
from the
presence of the cacti

244
00:25:17,006 --> 00:25:19,009
During four
weeks of the summer

245
00:25:19,049 --> 00:25:21,972
the sawaros
bloom at night

246
00:25:22,053 --> 00:25:24,256
to attract visitors

247
00:25:27,787 --> 00:25:31,231
The pollen and
nectar with which these flowers are loaded

248
00:25:31,271 --> 00:25:35,236
attract longnosed
and longtongued bats

249
00:25:50,716 --> 00:25:53,883
The bats left
Mexico a few days earlier

250
00:25:53,923 --> 00:25:55,967
to escape the
heat of summer

251
00:25:56,007 --> 00:25:57,611
and are on
their way north

252
00:25:57,651 --> 00:25:59,974
to the southern United States

253
00:26:02,180 --> 00:26:03,062
To get there

254
00:26:03,102 --> 00:26:06,107
they have to
cross the Sonoran Desert

255
00:26:12,126 --> 00:26:14,128
But the
desert is so big

256
00:26:14,168 --> 00:26:15,650
That for
most of the year

257
00:26:15,690 --> 00:26:17,932
They would be
unable to cross it

258
00:26:18,053 --> 00:26:18,574
Now

259
00:26:18,614 --> 00:26:20,536
with the
sowara in bloom

260
00:26:20,616 --> 00:26:23,059
They can
refuel on the way

261
00:26:33,282 --> 00:26:37,363
So the Soweto's
success in developing a way to store water

262
00:26:37,403 --> 00:26:41,003
is now crucial to
most of the animals that live

263
00:26:41,043 --> 00:26:43,723
or even travel
through this land

264
00:27:23,967 --> 00:27:28,689
The scarcity of rain determined the shape of this icon of the desert

265
00:27:28,729 --> 00:27:29,569
But water

266
00:27:29,609 --> 00:27:30,970
scarcely
though it is

267
00:27:31,010 --> 00:27:31,930
has also

268
00:27:31,970 --> 00:27:32,971
like the wind

269
00:27:33,011 --> 00:27:35,131
shaped the
land itself

270
00:27:52,251 --> 00:27:54,333
In the
deserts of Utah

271
00:27:54,373 --> 00:27:57,536
Ancient rivers
flowing across sandstone country

272
00:27:57,576 --> 00:28:00,059
steadily
widened their canyons

273
00:28:00,099 --> 00:28:00,980
Until now

274
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:02,381
the land
between them

275
00:28:02,421 --> 00:28:06,104
has been reduced
to spires and pinnacles

276
00:28:36,153 --> 00:28:41,433
With little or no
soil to retain the water on the surface of the land

277
00:28:41,473 --> 00:28:44,553
life here is
scarce indeed

278
00:28:46,353 --> 00:28:48,432
And when
resources are limited

279
00:28:48,472 --> 00:28:50,992
conflict is
never far away

280
00:29:01,668 --> 00:29:04,389
These are
Nubian ibexes

281
00:29:04,429 --> 00:29:07,029
and they're
squaring up for a duel

282
00:29:13,469 --> 00:29:15,150
And when
trouble starts

283
00:29:15,190 --> 00:29:18,030
a smart ibex
knows that the best thing to do

284
00:29:18,070 --> 00:29:20,110
is to gain
higher ground

285
00:29:37,921 --> 00:29:41,365
These are actually subordinate male ibex

286
00:29:41,405 --> 00:29:43,968
but their fights
are none the less serious

287
00:29:44,008 --> 00:29:47,853
Losing one might
mean never getting the chance to breed

288
00:29:47,893 --> 00:29:48,813
ever

289
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:13,903
When competitors
are evenly matched

290
00:30:13,943 --> 00:30:15,264
as they are here

291
00:30:15,304 --> 00:30:17,906
duels can
last for an hour

292
00:30:37,966 --> 00:30:39,288
In this heat

293
00:30:39,328 --> 00:30:42,292
the effort is
truly exhausting

294
00:30:44,337 --> 00:30:45,539
But victory here

295
00:30:45,579 --> 00:30:47,943
will gain
important ranking points

296
00:30:47,983 --> 00:30:50,226
On a male's
way to the top

297
00:31:22,093 --> 00:31:23,734
There's so
much at stake

298
00:31:23,774 --> 00:31:25,855
That not
all play fair

299
00:31:41,378 --> 00:31:43,861
The battle has
produced a winner

300
00:31:43,901 --> 00:31:47,545
But the ultimate
prize is not his yet

301
00:31:51,430 --> 00:31:55,474
That currently
belongs to the dominant male ibex

302
00:31:58,078 --> 00:32:02,043
His rank earns
him the loyalty of a harem of females

303
00:32:02,083 --> 00:32:03,685
And they
follow him closely

304
00:32:03,725 --> 00:32:05,807
As he travels
across this desert

305
00:32:05,847 --> 00:32:08,049
Searching for
food and water

306
00:32:10,268 --> 00:32:13,347
He doesn't have
to waste time looking for mates

307
00:32:13,387 --> 00:32:15,067
They're his
for the taking

308
00:32:15,107 --> 00:32:17,467
So he can
concentrate with them

309
00:32:17,507 --> 00:32:19,827
On keeping
fit and healthy

310
00:32:35,357 --> 00:32:38,518
Lizards are
desert specialists

311
00:32:41,601 --> 00:32:45,162
But here their
numbers are extraordinary

312
00:32:50,806 --> 00:32:53,088
These crevices
in South Africa

313
00:32:53,128 --> 00:32:56,649
contain the highest density of lizards in the world

314
00:33:06,884 --> 00:33:10,645
They're called
flat lizards for obvious reasons

315
00:33:10,685 --> 00:33:13,126
And they flaunt
their multicoloured bellies

316
00:33:13,166 --> 00:33:15,526
In territorial disputes

317
00:33:18,167 --> 00:33:19,887
He's made
his point

318
00:33:19,928 --> 00:33:22,648
And now it's time
to find some food

319
00:33:24,649 --> 00:33:26,170
As the
day warms up

320
00:33:26,210 --> 00:33:28,530
The lizards move
away from their cracks

321
00:33:28,570 --> 00:33:31,211
And head down to
the bottom of the gorge

322
00:33:32,492 --> 00:33:34,812
Their goal
is the river

323
00:33:37,185 --> 00:33:39,627
There is no
food at the edge

324
00:33:39,667 --> 00:33:43,109
But this desert
river holds a secret

325
00:33:49,835 --> 00:33:50,956
Each day

326
00:33:50,996 --> 00:33:55,079
black fly rise
from turbulent stretches of the river

327
00:33:55,479 --> 00:33:58,201
This is what the
lizards have come for

328
00:34:13,570 --> 00:34:15,732
The blackfly never land

329
00:34:15,772 --> 00:34:19,136
so the lizards
have to leap for their food

330
00:34:31,730 --> 00:34:32,730
In one day

331
00:34:32,770 --> 00:34:35,330
each of these
acrobatic little lizards

332
00:34:35,370 --> 00:34:37,650
may catch 50 flies

333
00:34:48,369 --> 00:34:50,729
There are plenty
of flies to go round

334
00:34:50,769 --> 00:34:53,969
even with hundreds of lizards competing for them

335
00:35:08,247 --> 00:35:09,967
Away from
these rapids

336
00:35:10,007 --> 00:35:12,006
flat lizard
populations

337
00:35:12,046 --> 00:35:14,446
are found in
much smaller numbers

338
00:35:14,486 --> 00:35:15,366
But here

339
00:35:15,406 --> 00:35:19,085
one unusual
abundance has produced another

340
00:35:30,657 --> 00:35:33,660
Deserts are
created by the lack of water

341
00:35:33,700 --> 00:35:36,183
But what actually
kills animals here

342
00:35:36,223 --> 00:35:38,225
is not
heat or thirst

343
00:35:38,265 --> 00:35:39,906
but lack of food

344
00:35:58,513 --> 00:36:00,554
So，how on earth

345
00:36:00,594 --> 00:36:02,715
does a plant
eater this size

346
00:36:02,755 --> 00:36:04,316
survive in a place

347
00:36:04,356 --> 00:36:08,238
apparently
totally devoid of vegetation？

348
00:36:14,882 --> 00:36:16,643
Elephants
in Namibia

349
00:36:16,683 --> 00:36:19,004
are the
toughest in Africa

350
00:36:19,045 --> 00:36:20,806
and they
need to be

351
00:36:38,097 --> 00:36:41,417
What little food
exists is so dispersed

352
00:36:41,457 --> 00:36:44,737
that these elephants walk up to 50 miles a day

353
00:36:44,777 --> 00:36:47,856
As they travel up
the dry river channels

354
00:36:47,896 --> 00:36:49,976
searching for
something to eat

355
00:37:05,272 --> 00:37:09,393
At times the task
looks truly hopeless

356
00:37:44,236 --> 00:37:48,084
Elephants may
seem out of place in this landscape

357
00:37:48,124 --> 00:37:49,164
但係佢哋唔係唯一嘅

358
00:38:07,181 --> 00:38:08,342
Amazingly

359
00:38:08,382 --> 00:38:10,503
lions
live here too

360
00:38:15,387 --> 00:38:16,869
In savannah country

361
00:38:16,909 --> 00:38:19,591
huge herds of
game support prides

362
00:38:19,631 --> 00:38:22,312
containing
twenty lions or more

363
00:38:22,393 --> 00:38:23,674
But to live here

364
00:38:23,714 --> 00:38:26,436
lions have had
to change their habits

365
00:38:26,476 --> 00:38:28,078
Prides are
much smaller

366
00:38:28,118 --> 00:38:31,080
and their home
ranges are very much bigger

367
00:38:32,201 --> 00:38:34,442
And there's an
added problem

368
00:38:36,014 --> 00:38:39,255
Their food is
always on the move

369
00:38:48,302 --> 00:38:49,663
Like the elephants

370
00:38:49,703 --> 00:38:52,464
the lions must
travel great distances

371
00:38:52,504 --> 00:38:54,425
to find
enough to live on

372
00:39:10,007 --> 00:39:12,776
But lions can'
t go everywhere

373
00:39:12,816 --> 00:39:16,948
They won't attempt to cross this field of sand dunes

374
00:39:17,352 --> 00:39:19,155
And the
oryx know it

375
00:40:19,177 --> 00:40:23,336
The lions must wait for the oryx to leave the safety of the dunes

376
00:40:23,376 --> 00:40:26,415
which eventually
they must to find food and water

377
00:40:26,455 --> 00:40:29,095
And then the
lions will ambush them

378
00:41:34,703 --> 00:41:38,542
The elephants
have found some of their favourite food

379
00:41:45,701 --> 00:41:48,820
Grasses are the
staple diet of all elephants

380
00:41:48,860 --> 00:41:52,300
But this herd
concentrates on digging up the roots

381
00:41:52,340 --> 00:41:56,059
Which have more
nutrition and moisture than the stems

382
00:41:56,630 --> 00:41:58,191
It's the sort
of behaviour

383
00:41:58,231 --> 00:42:02,194
that can make all
the difference in a pace of serious shortages

384
00:42:03,156 --> 00:42:06,839
Yet all this can
change in an instant

385
00:42:24,007 --> 00:42:28,927
The fortunes of
many deserts are ruled by distant rains

386
00:42:28,967 --> 00:42:34,048
This water fell as rain in mountains more than a hundred miles away

387
00:42:39,921 --> 00:42:41,883
It's known as
a flash flood

388
00:42:41,923 --> 00:42:42,843
and called that

389
00:42:42,883 --> 00:42:46,126
because the water
may run for just a single day

390
00:42:46,367 --> 00:42:49,329
It's an event
that only happens once or twice a year

391
00:42:49,369 --> 00:42:50,610
at the most

392
00:43:11,852 --> 00:43:16,418
The sandy riverbed acts like a giant strip of blotting paper

393
00:43:16,458 --> 00:43:19,420
sucking up the
water as soon as it appears

394
00:43:29,313 --> 00:43:32,757
But every square
metre of soil moistened by this river

395
00:43:32,797 --> 00:43:35,320
will increase the
chances of survival

396
00:43:35,360 --> 00:43:37,161
for those
that live here

397
00:43:42,858 --> 00:43:46,257
Water holes are
filled temporarily

398
00:43:51,016 --> 00:43:52,296
Elsewhere
in Africa

399
00:43:52,336 --> 00:43:54,535
elephants
drink every day

400
00:43:54,575 --> 00:43:56,175
But the lack
of water here

401
00:43:56,215 --> 00:43:59,694
means that desert
elephants can only refill their tanks

402
00:43:59,734 --> 00:44:02,454
once every
four or five days

403
00:44:04,081 --> 00:44:05,122
Within a week

404
00:44:05,162 --> 00:44:08,527
the flash flood
has produced a flush of green

405
00:44:08,567 --> 00:44:12,411
More than enough
to draw the oryx out of the dunes

406
00:44:14,775 --> 00:44:18,660
It's a rare chance for them to build up their food reserves

407
00:44:27,355 --> 00:44:31,477
The flood has
made life easier for the lions too

408
00:44:34,279 --> 00:44:39,321
The flesh of this oryx will keep the family going for a week at the most

409
00:44:39,521 --> 00:44:40,481
But for a while

410
00:44:40,521 --> 00:44:42,122
the hunting
will be easier

411
00:44:42,162 --> 00:44:44,883
Now that the
river channel has turned green

412
00:44:49,185 --> 00:44:52,269
The good times
for lions and oryx are brief

413
00:44:52,309 --> 00:44:54,312
But these are
the short moments

414
00:44:54,352 --> 00:44:58,196
That make it possible to live in deserts the year round

415
00:45:04,324 --> 00:45:07,488
Death Valley is
the hottest place on earth

416
00:45:07,528 --> 00:45:11,532
Yet even this
furnace can be transformed by water

417
00:45:22,408 --> 00:45:27,407
A single shower can enable seeds that have lain dormant for 30 years or more

418
00:45:27,447 --> 00:45:29,527
to burst
into life

419
00:45:38,047 --> 00:45:42,054
And there hasn't
been a bloom like this one for a century

420
00:45:54,645 --> 00:45:58,286
The periods of
boom in Death Valley are short

421
00:45:58,366 --> 00:46:02,367
But they're just
frequent enough to keep life ticking over

422
00:46:11,290 --> 00:46:13,451
A sudden flush
of vegetation

423
00:46:13,491 --> 00:46:16,011
Is what every
desert dweller waits for

424
00:46:16,051 --> 00:46:17,252
And when
it happens

425
00:46:17,292 --> 00:46:19,252
they must make
the most of it

426
00:46:39,707 --> 00:46:42,227
There is no other
species on the planet

427
00:46:42,267 --> 00:46:44,868
That responds as
quickly and as dramatically

428
00:46:44,908 --> 00:46:46,389
to the good times

429
00:46:46,429 --> 00:46:48,669
as the
desert locust

430
00:46:49,610 --> 00:46:52,859
Eggs that have
remained in the ground for twenty years

431
00:46:52,899 --> 00:46:54,301
begin to hatch

432
00:47:07,208 --> 00:47:09,492
The young locusts
are known as hoppers

433
00:47:09,532 --> 00:47:11,975
for at this stage
they're flightless

434
00:47:12,176 --> 00:47:14,580
They find new
feeding grounds

435
00:47:14,620 --> 00:47:18,265
by following the
smell of sprouting grass

436
00:47:24,602 --> 00:47:25,282
Normally

437
00:47:25,322 --> 00:47:28,642
it takes four
weeks for hoppers to become adults

438
00:47:28,682 --> 00:47:30,282
But when the
conditions are right

439
00:47:30,322 --> 00:47:31,242
as now

440
00:47:31,282 --> 00:47:34,842
their development
switches to the fast track

441
00:47:40,083 --> 00:47:43,323
As the vegetation
in one place begins to run out

442
00:47:43,363 --> 00:47:45,323
The winged adults
release pheromones

443
00:47:45,363 --> 00:47:46,963
scent messages

444
00:47:47,003 --> 00:47:50,363
which tell others
in the group that they must move on

445
00:48:02,553 --> 00:48:04,315
And when
groups merge

446
00:48:04,355 --> 00:48:06,477
they form a swarm

447
00:48:29,168 --> 00:48:33,292
An adult locust
eats its entire body weight every day

448
00:48:33,332 --> 00:48:38,695
and a whole swarm
can consume literally hundreds of tons of vegetation

449
00:48:40,537 --> 00:48:42,818
They have to
keep on moving

450
00:48:44,220 --> 00:48:46,582
The swarm
travels with the wind

451
00:48:46,622 --> 00:48:49,743
It's the most
energysaving way of flying

452
00:48:53,586 --> 00:48:55,429
Following the
flow of wind

453
00:48:55,469 --> 00:48:59,277
means that they'
re always heading toward areas of low pressure

454
00:48:59,317 --> 00:49:01,681
Places where
wind meets rain

455
00:49:01,721 --> 00:49:04,365
and vegetation
starts to grow

456
00:49:06,567 --> 00:49:07,729
As they fly

457
00:49:07,769 --> 00:49:10,332
swarms join up
with other swarms

458
00:49:10,372 --> 00:49:14,377
to form gigantic
plagues several billion strong

459
00:49:14,417 --> 00:49:16,979
and as much as
40 miles wide

460
00:49:19,063 --> 00:49:21,626
They will consume
every edible thing

461
00:49:21,666 --> 00:49:23,548
that lies
in their path

462
00:49:37,071 --> 00:49:40,952
This is one of
planet Earth's greatest spectacles

463
00:49:41,233 --> 00:49:43,434
It's rarely
seen on this scale

464
00:49:43,474 --> 00:49:45,315
and it won'
t last long

465
00:49:45,636 --> 00:49:47,317
Once the
food has gone

466
00:49:47,357 --> 00:49:51,319
the steady roar
of a billion beating locust wings

467
00:49:51,359 --> 00:49:53,400
Will once
again be replaced

468
00:49:53,440 --> 00:49:55,802
By nothing more
than the sound

469
00:49:55,842 --> 00:49:57,482
Of the desert wind

